<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Strada Nuova</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1550</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Via Garibaldi (Genoa)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Strada Nuova, now Via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>, is a street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> laid out from 1550 for the city's leading noble families, who built a row of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> palaces along it. Many of these houses were on the Rolli, the official lists of private palaces required to lodge visiting dignitaries, and in 2006 the street and the Rolli palaces were inscribed as a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/strada-nuova.jpg" alt="Strada Nuova" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Twice25 &amp; Rinina25 · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Genova-DSCF7468.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
